  L('m2c4-1', 'm2c4', 'Decision trees: classification and regression trees',
`A **decision tree** splits the data with a sequence of yes/no questions on features ("debt/income > 0.4?") until it reaches leaves.

• **Classification tree**: leaf predicts a class (majority vote). Splits chosen to maximise **purity**, measured by **Gini impurity** (1 − Σpᵢ²) or **entropy** (−Σpᵢ log₂pᵢ); the gain in purity is the **information gain**.
• **Regression tree**: leaf predicts a number (mean of the leaf). Splits chosen to minimise the **sum of squared errors / variance**.

Construction is **greedy and recursive**: at each node, test all features and thresholds, take the best split, repeat on each child until a stopping rule (max depth, min samples per leaf).

**Interpretation**: follow the path from root to leaf; very readable for small trees. Trees handle non-linearity and interactions and need no scaling, but deep trees overfit and are unstable (small data changes → different tree).`,
['Classification tree: Gini / entropy, majority vote', 'Regression tree: minimise squared error, mean in leaf', 'Greedy recursive splitting', 'Readable, no scaling, but overfit and unstable']);
  Q('m2c4-1', 'A node contains 8 defaulters and 2 non-defaulters. What is its Gini impurity?',
    ['0.16', '0.32', '0.50', '0.80'], 1,
    'Gini = 1 − (0.8² + 0.2²) = 1 − (0.64 + 0.04) = 0.32.');
  Q('m2c4-1', 'Which criterion does a regression tree typically use to choose splits?',
    ['Gini impurity', 'Reduction in the sum of squared errors (variance) of the target', 'Silhouette score', 'Log-likelihood of a logistic model'], 1,
    'Regression trees predict leaf means and pick the split that most reduces squared error.');
  Q('m2c4-1', 'What is the entropy of a node with a 50/50 class split?',
    ['0', '0.5', '1', '2'], 2,
    'Entropy = −(0.5·log₂0.5 + 0.5·log₂0.5) = 1, the maximum for two classes.');

  L('m2c4-2', 'm2c4', 'Pruning and ensemble techniques',
`**Pruning** fights overfitting of single trees.
• **Pre-pruning** (early stopping): limit max depth, minimum samples per leaf, minimum gain.
• **Post-pruning**: grow a full tree, then cut back branches that do not improve validation performance (**cost-complexity pruning**).

**Ensembles** combine many trees:
• **Bagging** (bootstrap aggregating): train trees on bootstrap samples and average/vote → **reduces variance**.
• **Random forest**: bagging plus a **random subset of features** at each split, which decorrelates trees. Gives feature importance and out-of-bag error.
• **Boosting** (AdaBoost, gradient boosting, XGBoost): trees built **sequentially**, each fixing the errors of the previous ones → mainly **reduces bias**; powerful but can overfit, so tune learning rate and number of trees.

Trade-off: ensembles are more accurate but less interpretable than one tree.`,
['Pre-pruning vs post-pruning (cost-complexity)', 'Bagging/random forest: parallel, reduce variance', 'Boosting: sequential, reduce bias', 'Ensembles: accuracy up, interpretability down']);
  Q('m2c4-2', 'What distinguishes a random forest from plain bagging of trees?',
