  L('m2c4-4', 'm2c4', 'Support vector machines (SVM)',
`An **SVM** finds the separating **hyperplane** with the **largest margin** between two classes.

• **Support vectors** are the training points closest to the boundary; only they determine the hyperplane.
• **Hard margin**: no errors allowed (only for perfectly separable data).
• **Soft margin**: allows some points inside the margin or misclassified, controlled by the **C** parameter. Large C → few violations, narrow margin, risk of overfitting; small C → wider margin, more tolerance, more bias.
• **Kernel trick**: implicitly maps data to a higher-dimensional space where a linear separator exists (polynomial, **radial basis function (RBF)** kernels), giving non-linear boundaries without computing the mapping explicitly.

Pros: effective in high dimensions, robust with clear margins. Cons: needs scaling, tuning of C and kernel parameters, poor probability outputs, slow on very large data.`,
['Maximum-margin hyperplane', 'Support vectors define the boundary', 'C: trade-off margin width vs violations', 'Kernel trick (RBF, polynomial) for non-linear boundaries']);
  Q('m2c4-4', 'In an SVM, which observations determine the position of the separating hyperplane?',
