  L('m2c4-6', 'm2c4', 'Advanced structures: CNNs and RNNs',
`**Convolutional neural networks (CNNs)** are designed for grid-like data such as images.
• **Convolutional layers** slide small filters (kernels) over the input to detect local patterns (edges, shapes); weights are **shared**, so far fewer parameters.
• **Pooling** layers (e.g. max-pooling) downsample and add some translation invariance.
• Uses: image recognition, document/cheque processing, satellite imagery, even chart patterns or time series.

**Recurrent neural networks (RNNs)** handle **sequences** (text, time series). A hidden state is passed from one time step to the next, acting as memory.
• Plain RNNs suffer from **vanishing gradients**, so they forget long-range dependencies.
• **LSTM** and **GRU** cells use gates (input, forget, output) to keep information over long sequences.
• Uses: time-series forecasting, transaction sequences for fraud, language.`,
['CNN: filters, weight sharing, pooling → images', 'RNN: hidden state carries memory → sequences', 'Vanishing gradients in plain RNNs', 'LSTM/GRU gates keep long-term memory']);
