  L('m2c4-7', 'm2c4', 'Autoencoders for dimensionality reduction (vs PCA)',
`An **autoencoder** is a neural network trained to **reproduce its own input**. It has:
• an **encoder** that compresses the input into a small **bottleneck** (latent representation),
• a **decoder** that reconstructs the input from the bottleneck.

It is trained by minimising **reconstruction error** — no labels needed (unsupervised / self-supervised). The bottleneck gives a compressed representation; a high reconstruction error flags **anomalies** (fraud, unusual trades).

**Autoencoder vs PCA**
• PCA is **linear**, has a closed-form solution, orthogonal and ordered components, cheap and more interpretable.
• Autoencoders with non-linear activations capture **non-linear** structure, but need more data, tuning and compute and are harder to interpret.
• A linear autoencoder with squared-error loss learns the same subspace as PCA.`,
['Encoder → bottleneck → decoder', 'Trained to minimise reconstruction error', 'High reconstruction error = anomaly', 'PCA linear & cheap; autoencoder non-linear & data hungry']);
