  L('m2c5-1', 'm2c5', 'How semi-supervised learning differs',
`**Semi-supervised learning (SSL)** sits between supervised and unsupervised learning: it trains on a **small labelled** set plus a **large unlabelled** set.

Why: labels are often expensive or slow to get (fraud investigations, defaults that take years to observe, manual document tagging), while unlabelled data is cheap.

• Supervised: only labelled data, ignores the unlabelled pool.
• Unsupervised: ignores labels entirely, finds structure only.
• Semi-supervised: uses the **structure in unlabelled data** to improve a predictor learned from few labels.

SSL only helps if the unlabelled data carries information about the labels — that is what the assumptions in the next objective are about. If assumptions fail, SSL can make the model **worse** than supervised learning on the labelled set alone.`,
['Few labels + many unlabelled examples', 'Labels costly, unlabelled data cheap', 'Uses structure of unlabelled data', 'Can hurt if assumptions fail']);
  Q('m2c5-1', 'Which statement BEST describes semi-supervised learning?',
    ['It needs no labels at all', 'It uses a small set of labelled data together with a large set of unlabelled data', 'It learns from rewards', 'It only works on images'], 1,
    'SSL combines a few labels with plentiful unlabelled data.');
  Q('m2c5-1', 'Compared with training only on the labelled subset, semi-supervised learning:',
    ['Always improves accuracy', 'Can improve accuracy, but may hurt performance if its assumptions about the data do not hold', 'Never changes the result', 'Removes the need for validation'], 1,
    'Benefits depend on the unlabelled data being informative under SSL assumptions; otherwise performance can degrade.');

  L('m2c5-2', 'm2c5', 'Assumptions required for effective semi-supervised learning',
`• **Smoothness (continuity) assumption**: points that are close to each other are likely to share the same label.
• **Cluster assumption**: data form clusters, and points in the same cluster tend to share a label; the decision boundary should pass through **low-density** regions (low-density separation).
• **Manifold assumption**: high-dimensional data lie on a lower-dimensional manifold; learning that structure from unlabelled data helps.

Also implicitly: labelled and unlabelled data come from the **same distribution**, and the labelled sample is representative.

If, for example, fraudsters deliberately mimic normal customers (so classes overlap heavily), the cluster assumption fails and SSL may spread wrong labels.`,
['Smoothness: nearby points share labels', 'Cluster / low-density separation', 'Manifold: data on lower-dimensional structure', 'Same distribution for labelled and unlabelled']);
  Q('m2c5-2', 'The "cluster assumption" in semi-supervised learning states that:',
