  L('m2c5-5', 'm2c5', 'Self-training versus co-training',
`**Self-training** (one model)
1. Train a classifier on the labelled data.
2. Predict the unlabelled data; add the **most confident** predictions as **pseudo-labels**.
3. Retrain and repeat.
+ Simple, works with any classifier. − **Confirmation bias**: early mistakes are reinforced and errors can accumulate.

**Co-training** (two models, two views)
1. Split features into two **views** that are each sufficient to predict the label and **conditionally independent** given the label (e.g. a web page's text vs the text of links pointing to it).
2. Train one classifier per view.
3. Each classifier labels its most confident unlabelled examples **for the other** one.
+ Less confirmation bias because the views make different mistakes. − Needs two genuinely independent, sufficient views, which are rare.`,
['Self-training: one model, pseudo-labels, confirmation bias', 'Co-training: two views, models teach each other', 'Co-training needs sufficient, conditionally independent views']);
  Q('m2c5-5', 'What is the main weakness of self-training?',
    ['It needs two independent feature views', 'Incorrect pseudo-labels can reinforce themselves (confirmation bias)', 'It cannot use unlabelled data', 'It only works for regression'], 1,
    'The model learns from its own confident predictions; early errors get added as labels and amplified.');
  Q('m2c5-5', 'Co-training requires which condition to work well?',
    ['A single feature', 'Two feature views that are each sufficient for prediction and conditionally independent given the label', 'Fully labelled data', 'A reward signal'], 1,
    'Each view must be able to predict on its own, and the views should provide independent evidence.');

