    'In a bandit, each decision is independent of state transitions; general RL involves sequences of states affected by actions.');
  Q('m2c6-3', 'In the bandit setting, "regret" measures:',
    ['The number of arms', 'The reward lost compared with always choosing the optimal arm', 'Model training time', 'The discount factor'], 1,
    'Regret is the cumulative shortfall versus the best possible arm, the cost of learning.');

  L('m2c6-4', 'm2c6', 'Monte Carlo versus Temporal Difference methods',
`Both estimate value functions from experience (model-free).

**Monte Carlo (MC)**
• Waits until the **end of an episode**, then updates values using the **actual total return** G.
• V(s) ← V(s) + α[G − V(s)].
• **Unbiased** but **high variance**; requires episodes that terminate.

**Temporal Difference (TD)**
• Updates after **every step** using the observed reward plus the **estimated value of the next state** (**bootstrapping**).
• TD(0): V(s) ← V(s) + α[r + γV(s') − V(s)]. The bracket is the **TD error**.
• Lower variance, learns online and in continuing tasks, but **biased** by the current estimates.
• **Q-learning** (off-policy) and **SARSA** (on-policy) are TD control methods.`,
['MC: wait for episode end, actual return, unbiased, high variance', 'TD: update each step, bootstrap, lower variance, biased', 'TD error = r + γV(s\') − V(s)', 'Q-learning (off-policy), SARSA (on-policy)']);
