  L('m2c7-1', 'm2c7', 'OLS, nonlinear least squares and maximum likelihood',
`• **Ordinary least squares (OLS)**: choose parameters that minimise the **sum of squared residuals** in a model that is **linear in the parameters**. Closed-form solution β = (X'X)⁻¹X'y.
• **Nonlinear least squares (NLS)**: same objective (squared residuals) but the model is **non-linear in the parameters** (e.g. y = a·e^(bx)). No closed form → iterative numerical optimisation (Gauss-Newton, gradient methods); risk of local minima, needs starting values.
• **Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)**: choose parameters that make the observed data **most probable** under an assumed distribution; maximise the (log-)likelihood. Used for logistic regression, GARCH, many ML models (minimising cross-entropy = MLE).

Link: with normally distributed errors, MLE for a linear regression gives the same coefficients as OLS.`,
['OLS: linear in parameters, closed form', 'NLS: non-linear, iterative, starting values', 'MLE: maximise (log-)likelihood under a distribution', 'Normal errors → MLE = OLS']);
