  L('m2c7-2', 'm2c7', 'Gradient descent for estimating parameters',
`**Gradient descent** minimises a loss function L(θ) step by step:

θ_new = θ_old − η · ∂L/∂θ

• The **gradient** points uphill; move in the opposite direction.
• **η (learning rate)** sets the step size: too large → overshoot/diverge; too small → very slow, may stall.
• Stop when the gradient is near zero or improvement is tiny (convergence).
• For convex losses (linear/logistic regression) it reaches the global minimum; for neural networks it may end in local minima or saddle points.

Example: L(θ) = (θ − 3)², gradient 2(θ − 3). From θ = 0 with η = 0.25: gradient −6, new θ = 0 − 0.25·(−6) = 1.5.`,
['θ ← θ − η·gradient', 'Learning rate too high diverges, too low is slow', 'Convex → global minimum; neural nets → local minima/saddles']);
