  L('m2c7-5', 'm2c7', 'Computational issues in training neural networks',
`• **Vanishing gradients**: gradients shrink as they are multiplied backwards through many layers (especially with sigmoid/tanh), so early layers barely learn.
• **Exploding gradients**: gradients grow uncontrollably (deep nets, RNNs) → unstable weights.
• **Local minima, saddle points and plateaus** in a non-convex loss surface.
• **Overfitting** with many parameters.
• **Cost**: long training times, need for GPUs, large memory.

**Solutions**
• **ReLU** (and variants) activations reduce vanishing gradients.
• Careful **weight initialisation** (Xavier/Glorot, He).
• **Batch normalisation** stabilises layer inputs.
• **Gradient clipping** against exploding gradients.
• **Residual (skip) connections**; LSTM/GRU for sequences.
• Adaptive optimisers (Adam), momentum, learning-rate schedules.
• Regularisation: dropout, weight decay, early stopping.`,
