  L('m2c7-10', 'm2c7', 'Cross-validation, stratified cross-validation and bootstrapping',
`• **k-fold cross-validation**: split data into k folds (e.g. 5 or 10). Train on k − 1 folds, validate on the remaining fold; repeat k times so each fold is used once for validation; average the scores. Uses data efficiently and gives a more stable performance estimate.
• **Leave-one-out (LOOCV)**: k = n; nearly unbiased but expensive and high variance.
• **Stratified k-fold**: each fold keeps the **same class proportions** as the full data set — essential for **imbalanced** targets (defaults, fraud).
• **Time-series CV** (rolling/expanding window): never train on the future.
• **Bootstrapping**: draw many samples of size n **with replacement**; fit/evaluate on each to estimate the variability of a statistic or model. About **63.2%** of observations appear in a bootstrap sample; the remaining ~36.8% are **out-of-bag** and can serve as validation (as in random forests).`,
